    "pressure": {
      "file": "journal_bearing_pressure.glb",
      "title": "Hydrodynamic pressure lifts the journal",
      "description": "When the shaft turns it drags oil into the narrowing gap on one side of the eccentric journal. The wedge builds pressure (bars) that floats the shaft on an oil film; the shaft settles at an eccentricity and attitude angle that balance the load. Minimum film thickness is typically ≈ 10–50 µm; the bearing clearance is ≈ 1–2 µm per mm of diameter. At start-up and shut-down there is little film, so most babbitt wear happens then (jacking oil is used on large machines).",
      "render": "normal",
      "annotations": []
    }
  },
